WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.149
Your instrument says 15,000
of 20,000 partitions are positive.

00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:09.840
How many copies are actually inside them,
and why can't the instrument tell you?

00:00:10.246 --> 00:00:14.871
15,000 is the tempting answer,
and it is not the answer.

00:00:14.871 --> 00:00:17.493
The reason starts with a rain shower.

00:00:17.493 --> 00:00:21.221
And one number you type
yourself scales every result,

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:25.086
and the calculator cannot
check it, so stay to the end.

00:00:27.357 --> 00:00:32.674
Picture a light shower falling
at random on 20,000 paving tiles,

00:00:32.674 --> 00:00:36.194
all the same size, each
drop landing on its own.

00:00:36.194 --> 00:00:39.140
You cannot count the drops on each tile.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.517
You can only see whether
a tile is wet or dry.

00:00:42.924 --> 00:00:46.379
As the rain goes on,
some tiles take one drop,

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:49.981
some take two, three or
more, and some stay dry.

00:00:49.981 --> 00:00:53.804
A wet tile does not say
how many drops made it wet.

00:00:54.212 --> 00:00:55.300
Now the clue.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.252
A quarter of the tiles are still dry.

00:00:58.252 --> 00:01:02.525
Because the drops fell at
random, that tells you that,

00:01:02.525 --> 00:01:07.652
on average, each tile
took about 1.386 drops.

00:01:08.059 --> 00:01:11.326
This works only if
the drops fall at random,

00:01:11.326 --> 00:01:15.464
each one independently,
and every tile is the same size.

00:01:15.464 --> 00:01:19.094
If drops come in clumps,
several land on one tile

00:01:19.094 --> 00:01:22.361
and the count from
the dry tiles is too low.

00:01:22.361 --> 00:01:26.499
In the lab, a tile is a partition,
and a drop is a copy.

00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:31.834
In the instrument's words,
a wet tile is a positive

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:34.429
partition and a dry
tile is a negative one.

00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:39.207
That call is made in your instrument's
analysis software, not by the calculator.

00:01:39.615 --> 00:01:41.642
Only two things are counted:

00:01:41.642 --> 00:01:46.535
5,000 negative partitions
and 15,000 positive ones.

00:01:46.941 --> 00:01:50.901
How many positives held
one copy, two, or three?

00:01:50.901 --> 00:01:52.679
Nobody measured that.

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.396
It is expected, if
the copies fell at random.

00:01:56.396 --> 00:01:58.901
Here are the expected numbers.

00:01:59.305 --> 00:02:03.301
If copies are clumped, or
joined on one DNA fragment,

00:02:03.301 --> 00:02:05.965
they sit together in one partition,

00:02:05.965 --> 00:02:09.665
so the real spread differs
and the result is low.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:15.583
Lambda, the average number of copies
per partition, is minus the natural

00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:21.322
log of 1 − p, where p is the fraction
of partitions that are positive.

00:02:21.322 --> 00:02:26.910
With p at 0.75, lambda is about 1.386.

00:02:29.679 --> 00:02:32.463
This is the Ligant Digital PCR Calculator.

00:02:32.463 --> 00:02:34.469
Click Load the worked example.

00:02:34.469 --> 00:02:40.554
The form fills in, with 15,000 positive
partitions out of 20,000 accepted.

00:02:40.554 --> 00:02:41.849
One flag is raised:

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:46.316
the worked example's 1 nL
partition volume is still in use.

00:02:46.316 --> 00:02:51.559
It is a round number for following the
arithmetic, not your instrument's volume.

00:02:53.021 --> 00:02:55.489
Step 1 draws the fraction positive:

00:02:55.489 --> 00:02:59.710
one dot for
every 100 partitions, 150 filled.

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:03.672
The positives are lined up first
only so you can count them.

00:03:03.672 --> 00:03:07.114
In a real run they are mixed
in among the negatives.

00:03:07.114 --> 00:03:10.101
It is a schematic, not
a picture of your run.

00:03:11.561 --> 00:03:14.150
Now the answer to the opening question,

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.097
in the chart How many copies
are in the positive partitions?

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:19.974
The outlined bar is counted.

00:03:19.974 --> 00:03:23.857
The filled bars are expected,
if the copies fell at random.

00:03:23.857 --> 00:03:31.881
About 27,726 copies estimated, all
in the 15,000 positive partitions.

00:03:38.562 --> 00:03:44.330
Step 2 draws the rule from before, lambda
equals minus the natural log of 1 − p.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:46.363
It is not fitted to your data.

00:03:46.363 --> 00:03:50.689
It stops at 99% positive
because that is the edge

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:53.442
of the drawing, not a limit on your data.

00:03:54.904 --> 00:03:59.995
Now predict. Positives
go from 15,000 to 18,000:

00:03:59.995 --> 00:04:01.545
20% more.

00:04:01.545 --> 00:04:04.496
By about how much will the result rise?

00:04:04.496 --> 00:04:05.824
Pause and decide.

00:04:12.231 --> 00:04:15.681
Type 18,000 into Positive partitions.

00:04:15.681 --> 00:04:17.371
Watch four things move:

00:04:17.371 --> 00:04:21.736
the disc, the dots, the counted
negative bar, and the result.

00:04:21.736 --> 00:04:30.184
115,129.3 copies per µL
of the original sample:

00:04:30.184 --> 00:04:33.071
about 66% more, not 20%.

00:04:35.534 --> 00:04:37.027
Check your prediction:

00:04:37.027 --> 00:04:41.894
each extra positive adds about
1 over the number of negatives to lambda.

00:04:46.854 --> 00:04:49.545
So why does the result climb faster?

00:04:49.545 --> 00:04:53.183
Early on, each new wet
tile means about one drop.

00:04:53.183 --> 00:04:57.111
Later, most drops land
on tiles that are already wet,

00:04:57.111 --> 00:05:00.020
so each newly wet tile stands for many.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:04.894
Again, this holds only for drops
falling at random on equal tiles.

00:05:05.804 --> 00:05:08.332
In Show the working, one box is dashed:

00:05:08.332 --> 00:05:09.722
the partition volume.

00:05:09.722 --> 00:05:13.135
You declare it; the calculator
has no default for it.

00:05:13.135 --> 00:05:17.053
Think of an exchange rate you
type into a currency converter:

00:05:17.053 --> 00:05:21.792
if your rate is wrong, every converted
amount is wrong by the same factor.

00:05:21.792 --> 00:05:25.268
Every result scales as
1 divided by partition volume.

00:05:25.268 --> 00:05:30.260
Declare 0.85 nL instead
of the example's 1 nL,

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:35.821
and every result is 1.176
times as high, about 18% more.

00:05:35.821 --> 00:05:39.044
The tool shows published
values with their sources

00:05:39.044 --> 00:05:42.204
but never chooses one,
and it cannot check yours.

00:05:43.665 --> 00:05:46.634
At the foot of the page, a scope box says

00:05:46.634 --> 00:05:49.745
what the tool does
and what it does not do.

00:05:51.656 --> 00:05:55.620
Remember: a positive partition
can hold more than one copy.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.395
Only negatives and positives are counted;

00:05:58.395 --> 00:06:01.567
the rest is expected, if
copies fall at random.

00:06:01.567 --> 00:06:04.937
The result climbs faster
as the negatives run out.

00:06:04.937 --> 00:06:07.976
And the partition volume
is yours to declare.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:09.928
Try it with your own run.

00:06:09.928 --> 00:06:14.214
The Ligant Digital PCR Calculator
is free, and it runs in your browser.

00:06:14.214 --> 00:06:17.012
The link is in the description.
Before you go:

00:06:17.012 --> 00:06:19.572
what did you predict for 18,000?

00:06:19.572 --> 00:06:21.060
Tell us in the comments.
